push adds one or more elements to the end of an array, while unshift adds them to the beginning. Both mutate the original array and return the new length.

code:
const arr = [2];
arr.push(3);
arr.unshift(1);
console.log(arr);  // [1, 2, 3]

real example:
Building an ordered list of test steps by appending with push keeps the execution order natural and easy to assert.

Use the spread operator [...arr] or slice(). Both create a new array so later mutations of the copy do not affect the original.

code:
const original = [1, 2];
const copy = [...original];
copy.push(3);
console.log(original);  // [1, 2]

real example:
Sorting a locator result set with [...items].sort() keeps the original DOM order intact for other assertions.

Compare against .length or use the truthiness of the array itself, since an empty array is truthy but has length 0.

code:
const rows = [];
if (rows.length === 0) console.log('no data');

real example:
Guard a for...of over locator results with a length check so a missing table does not silently promise and then fail later assertions.
